{:edb/db-before-t 2592 :edb/db-after-t 2593 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535905 50 #inst "2026-10-15T06:01:14.279Z" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186058878 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058882 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186059707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540075 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186059709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540100 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186060376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186060376 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x + 2} =-\\frac{4}{x}$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186060376 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186060376 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186064295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540078 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186075316 1078 #edb/ref 17592186075317 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078250 1071 "Find the value of $\\cos L$.\n\n![](images/78/78416d3c8ec8a8351ecfe0a4ea81feb05cce29f55dd9ec4bc676e6363dd49ec0.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078250 1071 "Find the value of $\\cos L.$\n\n![](images/78/78416d3c8ec8a8351ecfe0a4ea81feb05cce29f55dd9ec4bc676e6363dd49ec0.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078250 1074 "Recall the definition of cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac {\\textrm{adjacent}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $KL$. The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} KL &= \\sqrt{JK^2 + JL^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(3)^2 + (3\\sqrt3)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{9 + 27} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{36} \\\\ &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\cos L &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 3}{6} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078250 1074 "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac {\\textrm{adjacent}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $KL.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nKL &= \\sqrt{ JK^2 + JL^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ (3)^2 + (3\\sqrt3)^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{  9 + 27 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{36}\\\n&= 6\\\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\cos L &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL} \\[5pt] \n&= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 3}{6} \\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2.\n}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078252 1071 "Find the sine of $\\angle Y$.\n\n![](images/e0/e0fb47c85d56c314389a0a334029b3b769f13ddcca6ab15a9021cf6361e4b1ce.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078252 1071 "Find the sine of $\\angle Y.$\n\n![](images/e0/e0fb47c85d56c314389a0a334029b3b769f13ddcca6ab15a9021cf6361e4b1ce.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078252 1074 "Recall the definition of sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin Y = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {XZ}{YZ} = \\dfrac{XZ}{61}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XZ$. The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} XZ &= \\sqrt{YZ^2 - XY^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{61^2 - 60^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{3721- 3600} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{121} \\\\ &= 11 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin Y&= \\dfrac{XZ}{61} = \\dfrac{11}{61}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078252 1074 "Recall the definition of sine: \n\n$$\n\\sin Y = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {XZ}{YZ} = \\dfrac{XZ}{61}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XZ.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nXZ &= \\sqrt{ YZ^2 - XY^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 61^2 - 60^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 3721- 3600 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{121 }\\\n&= 11\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin Y&= \\dfrac{XZ}{61} = \\dfrac{11}{61}.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078254 1071 "Calculate $\\tan K$.\n\n![](images/fb/fbac8700b860cbe8a60f2c24a5ab7ee9e7adfda584b1077edf208b0dcd16744e.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078254 1071 "Calculate $\\tan K.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbac8700b860cbe8a60f2c24a5ab7ee9e7adfda584b1077edf208b0dcd16744e.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078254 1074 "Recall the definition of tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan K = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{adjacent}} = \\dfrac {JL}{JK} = \\dfrac{JL}{7.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $JL$. The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} JL &= \\sqrt{KL^2 - JK^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{12.5^2 - 7.5^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{156.25-56.25} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{100} \\\\ &= 10 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\tan K&= \\dfrac{JL}{7.5} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{10}{7.5} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac 4 3. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078254 1074 "Recall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\tan K = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{adjacent}} = \\dfrac {JL}{JK} = \\dfrac{JL}{7.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $JL.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nJL &= \\sqrt{ KL^2 - JK^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 12.5^2 - 7.5^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 156.25-56.25 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{100 }\\\n&= 10\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\tan K&= \\dfrac{JL}{7.5}\\[5pt]\n &= \\dfrac{10}{7.5}  \\[5pt]\n &=\\dfrac 4 3.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078264 1071 "Find $\\csc\\theta$, $\\sec\\theta$, and $\\cot\\theta$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/34/343917ea041d51cac38619f3577765eee7fbc3ea85e175148b6c591e11ae230b.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078264 1071 "Find $\\csc\\theta,$ $\\sec\\theta,$ and $\\cot\\theta$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/34/343917ea041d51cac38619f3577765eee7fbc3ea85e175148b6c591e11ae230b.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078264 1074 "We compute the three reciprocal trigonometric ratios as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\csc\\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac 5 3, \\\\[5pt] \\sec\\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac 5 4, \\\\[5pt] \\cot\\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta} = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac 4 3. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078264 1074 "We compute the three reciprocal trigonometric ratios as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\csc\\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac 5 3, \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sec\\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta} =  \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac 5 4, \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cot\\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta} = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac 4 3.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078265 1074 "Remember that $\\sec$ is the reciprocal of $\\cos$. So first we need to compute cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{JL}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JL$, we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJL &= \\sqrt{KL^{2} - JK^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5^{2} - 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25 - 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{JL}{5} = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec L &= (\\frac{4}{5})^{-1} = \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078265 1074 "Remember that $\\sec$ is the reciprocal of $\\cos.$ So first we need to compute cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{JL}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JL,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JL & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{K}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{JL}{5} = \\dfrac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡L & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540063 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540105 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540081 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540033 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540061 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540114 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540049 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540101 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540062 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540074 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078641 1050 420.0 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078657 1050 780.0 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078740 1071 "![](images/f9/f9f59d4328ad43e6e342da3cf9e87ccd5dfe33b97c46424e7fcc2b9f562fc452.png)\n\nFind the tangent of $∠N$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186078740 1071 "![](images/f9/f9f59d4328ad43e6e342da3cf9e87ccd5dfe33b97c46424e7fcc2b9f562fc452.png)\n\nFind the tangent of $∠N.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186078740 1074 "Recall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡N=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{OM}{MN}=\\frac{OM}{2.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $OM.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OM & =\\sqrt{O{N}^{2}-M{N}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6.5}^{2}-{2.5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{42.25-6.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡N & =\\frac{OM}{2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186079637 1064 "Suppose that we have a right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ with legs $AC= BC = 1$, like the one in the diagram below.\nHow can we find the value $\\sin \\theta$ if we don't know one of the relevant sides?\n\n![](images/ae/ae5157e6824fd98861194302ab912e76d9e79dade6706dfbedea97d859f18a79.png)\n\nRecall the definition of sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {AC}{AB} = \\dfrac 1 c\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $c$. The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= \\sqrt{AC^{2} + BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish computing the value of $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\frac{1}{c} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo matter which trigonometric function we need to find, if we are missing one of the relevant sides but we know the lengths of the other two sides, we can always use the Pythagorean Theorem." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186079637 1064 "Suppose that we have a right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ with legs $AC= BC = 1,$ like the one in the diagram below.\nHow can we find the value $\\sin \\theta$ if we don't know one of the relevant sides?\n\n![](images/ae/ae5157e6824fd98861194302ab912e76d9e79dade6706dfbedea97d859f18a79.png)\n\nRecall the definition of sine: \n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {AC}{AB} = \\dfrac 1 c\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $c.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{A{C}^{2}+B{C}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish computing the value of $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{c}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo matter which trigonometric function we need to find, if we are missing one of the relevant sides but we know the lengths of the other two sides, we can always use the Pythagorean Theorem." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186079643 1064 "The reciprocals of sine, cosine, and tangent are given special names:\n\n- The reciprocal of **sine** is **cosecant**, abbreviated $\\csc$.\n$\\csc(\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\sin(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}}$\n- The reciprocal of **cosine** is **secant**, abbreviated $\\sec$.\n$\\sec (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\cos(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}}$\n- The reciprocal of **tangent** is **cotangent**, abbreviated $\\cot$.\n$\\cot (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\tan(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{adjacent}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}}$\n\nLet's write down expressions for $\\csc(B)$, $\\sec(B)$, and $\\cot(B)$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e9/e98dcc332739ff381e8dc43160a907e471062c5b8a75567ba7dbb8687c008efa.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\csc(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac c b \\\\[5pt] \\sec(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac c a \\\\[5pt] \\cot(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac a b \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186079643 1064 "The reciprocals of sine, cosine, and tangent are given special names:\n\n- The reciprocal of **sine** is **cosecant**, abbreviated $\\csc.$\n\n$$\n\\csc(\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\sin(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}}\n$$\n\n- The reciprocal of **cosine** is **secant**, abbreviated $\\sec.$\n\n$$\n\\sec (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\cos(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}}\n$$\n\n- The reciprocal of **tangent** is **cotangent**, abbreviated $\\cot.$\n\n$$\n\\cot (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\tan(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{adjacent}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}}\n$$\n\nLet's write down expressions for $\\csc(B),$ $\\sec(B),$ and $\\cot(B)$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e9/e98dcc332739ff381e8dc43160a907e471062c5b8a75567ba7dbb8687c008efa.png)\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\csc(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac c b \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sec(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac c a \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cot(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac a b \\\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186079644 1064 "If we are given two sides of a right triangle, how can we calculate the reciprocal ratios $\\sec$, $\\csc$, or $\\cot$ when we are missing one of the necessary sides in the ratio?\n\nThe trick here is the same that we use for finding $\\sin$, $\\cos$, or $\\tan$: we use the Pythagorean theorem for finding the missing side.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $\\csc L$ in the triangle below.\n\n![](images/9f/9f2e4ea4922783c84a6cb59c5e1532b726d46c9a837a5b1eaa203bbdc6cc1741.png)\n\nRemember that $\\csc$ is the reciprocal of $\\sin$. So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin L = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JK}{KL} = \\dfrac{JK}{4}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JK$, we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJK &= \\sqrt{KL^{2} - JL^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} - 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 - 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\sin L = \\dfrac{JK}{4} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc L &= (\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7} \\cdot \\sqrt{7}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186079644 1064 "If we are given two sides of a right triangle, how can we calculate the reciprocal ratios $\\sec,$ $\\csc,$ or $\\cot$ when we are missing one of the necessary sides in the ratio?\n\nThe trick here is the same that we use for finding $\\sin,$ $\\cos,$ or $\\tan\\mathbin{:}$ we use the Pythagorean theorem for finding the missing side.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $\\csc L$  in the triangle below. \n\n![](images/9f/9f2e4ea4922783c84a6cb59c5e1532b726d46c9a837a5b1eaa203bbdc6cc1741.png)\n\nRemember that $\\csc$ is the reciprocal of $\\sin.$ So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin L = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JK}{KL} = \\dfrac{JK}{4}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JK,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JK & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{L}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\sin L = \\dfrac{JK}{4} = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{7} }{4},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡L & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186082942 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=2x^2-12x+3$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186082942 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=2x^2-12x+3.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186082942 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=2$ and $b=-12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= - ((-12))/(2(2)) \\\\\n&= - ((-12))/(4) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186082942 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=2$ and $b=-12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-12)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-12)}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186082943 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-x^2+4x+1$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186082943 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-x^2+4x+1.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186082943 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry.\n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= - (4)/(2(-1)) \\\\\n&= - (4)/((-2)) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=2$. This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x^{2} + 4x + 1 \\\\\n&= - (2)^{2} + 4(2) + 1 \\\\\n&=-4 + 8 + 1 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(2,5)$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186082943 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+4x+1 \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+8+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(2,5).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186082944 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=0.25x^2+kx+2$ is $x=-6$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186082944 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=0.25x^2+kx+2$ is $x=-6.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186082944 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k$. Then, we will set our result equal to $-6$.\n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=0.25$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= - (k)/(2(0.25)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{k}{0.5} \\\\\n&=-2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-6$, so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2k &= -6 \\\\\nk &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186082944 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $-6.$\n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=0.25$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(0.25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{0.5} \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-6,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540026 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540082 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540055 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540085 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540089 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540093 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540095 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540108 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186083410 1050 420.0 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186084524 1064 "To determine the axis of symmetry of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwe can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result in a separate lesson. For now, let's get some practice working with it.\n\nSuppose we want to find the axis of symmetry of the function\n\n$$\ny=3x^2-6x+1\n$$\n\nTo do this, we substitute $a=3$ and $b=-6$ into the above formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\\n&= - ((-6))/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is the equation of the axis of symmetry.\n\n![](images/88/88ee917eb387ee60e67080785519ffe9cb4333adcd602a733659d0447c37f61a.png)\n\nThe vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so $x=1$ also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=1$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 3x^{2} - 6x + 1 \\\\\n&= 3(1)^{2} - 6(1) + 1 \\\\\n&= 3 - 6 + 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(1,-2)$.\n\n![](images/a3/a391338ffb2bddd2a58b836e328c574d348215c518b27d9886ca11a21674943a.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186084524 1064 "To determine the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result in a separate lesson. For now, let's get some practice working with it. \n\nSuppose we want to find the axis of symmetry of the function $y=3x^2-6x+1.$ To do this, we substitute $a=3$ and $b=-6$ into the above formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-6)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is the equation of the axis of symmetry. \n\n![](images/88/88ee917eb387ee60e67080785519ffe9cb4333adcd602a733659d0447c37f61a.png)\n\nThe vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so $x=1$ also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=1$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}-6x+1 \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}-6(1)+1 \\\\  & =3-6+1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(1,-2).$\n\n![](images/a3/a391338ffb2bddd2a58b836e328c574d348215c518b27d9886ca11a21674943a.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186099963 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099965 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186100924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540035 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186100924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540077 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186100925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540079 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186100925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540086 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186101467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186101467 1071 "Suppose that\n$S = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n}n)/(n!)$\nand that $s_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S$. According to the alternating series error bound, $\\mid S - s_{5} \\mid < k$ for some real number $k$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186101467 1071 "Suppose that \n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}n}{n!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{5}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186101467 1074 "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n}{n!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}n}{n!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $5$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{5}|<|{b}_{6}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{6}\\cdot 6}{6!}|=\\frac{6}{720}=\\frac{1}{120}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{120}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186107941 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107942 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186107941 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107943 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [1759218610794