a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡7+3{log}_{5}⁡3 & =3({log}_{5}⁡7+{log}_{5}⁡3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186110488 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (x^{2}y) &= \\ln (x^{2} \\cdot y) \\\\\n&= \\ln (x^{2}) + \\ln y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186110488 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186110489 1071 "If $\\log_2(3)=k$ and $\\log_2(x)=p$, what is the value of $\\log_2(3x)$?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186110489 1071 "If $\\log_2(3)=k$ and $\\log_2(x)=p,$ what is the value of $\\log_2(3x)?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186110489 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} (3x) &= \\log_{2} 3 + \\log_{2} x \\\\\n&= k + p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186110489 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{2}⁡3+{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =k+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186110696 1038 #