$1\\,458 \\: \\textrm{cm}^3$ of empty space in the carton." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186119712 1074 "The orange juice in the carton creates a cube. Since the height of the juice is $9\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the side length of the cube is also $9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\nThe height of $9 \\: \\textrm{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents a third of the carton's height. Therefore, the height of the carton is\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 9 = 27 \\: \\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the carton is a rectangular prism that is $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ long, $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ wide, and $27 - 9 = 18\\:\\textrm{cm}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=18\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 9 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 18 = 1\\,458\\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there is $1\\,458 \\: \\textrm{cm}^3$ of empty space in the carton." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540030 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540046 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540060 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540088 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540064 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540070 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540072 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540022 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540047 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540071 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540102 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186119986 1050 420.0 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120060 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=7\\,\\text{in},\\,w=2\\,\\text{in},\\,h=3\\,\\text{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=7\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=42\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120088 1071 "A bar of chocolate in the form of a rectangular prism is $9\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $2\\,\\text{cm}$ high. If the chocolate costs $$0.02$ per cubic centimeter, what is the value of four identical bars of chocolate?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120088 1071 "A bar of chocolate in the form of a rectangular prism is $9cm$ long, $5cm$ wide, and $2cm$ high. If the chocolate costs $$0.02$ per cubic centimeter, what is the value of four identical bars of chocolate?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186120088 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of the bar of chocolate.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9,\\,w=5,\\,h=2\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=9\\cdot 5\\cdot 2=90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the four bars of chocolate is\n\n$$\n90\\cdot 4=360\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the cost of four chocolate bars is\n\n$$\n360\\cdot 0.02=$7.20.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120174 1071 "The height of a rectangular solid is $15\\,\\text{cm}$ and its volume is $675\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ If the base of the solid is a square, find the length of the solid." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186120174 1071 "The height of a rectangular solid is $15cm$ and its volume is $675cm^{3}$. If the base of the solid is a square, find the length of the solid." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186120174 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSince the base of the rectangular solid is a square, we have that \n\n$$\nl=w.\n$$\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=675,\\,h=15,\\,w=l\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =lwh \\\\ V & ={l}^{2}h \\\\ 675 & ={l}^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =45 \\\\ l & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186121085 1064 "Let's consider the rectangular prism below. How can we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/94/94bdf8333e1cb6a7ff9f99bc4ef854433bc99a8310f61181309cf51709e7569c.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume $V$ of a rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl = 20\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=10\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=5\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 20 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 5 = 1000cm^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186121085 1064 "Let's consider the rectangular prism below. How can we calculate its volume?  \n\n![](images/94/94bdf8333e1cb6a7ff9f99bc4ef854433bc99a8310f61181309cf51709e7569c.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume $V$ of a rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl = 20\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=10\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=5\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =20\\cdot 10\\cdot 5=1\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186156129 1078 #edb/ref 17592186156132 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540080 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540073 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540087 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540104 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157868 1071 "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $1000$ water bottles has $20$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find an upside down label before checking the $5$th bottle? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186157868 1071 "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $1\\,000$ water bottles has $20$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find an upside down label before checking the $5$th bottle? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186157868 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bottles checked until a defect is found. On average, the chance of finding an upside down label on any water bottle is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{20}{1\\,000}=0.02.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of bottles that the inspector checks before finding an upside down label can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.02.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.02).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.02)^{x-1}(0.02) \\\\  & =(0.98)^{x-1}(0.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding an upside down label before checking the $5$th bottle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.98)^{1-1}(0.02)+(0.98)^{2-1}(0.02)+(0.98)^{3-1}(0.02)+(0.98)^{4-1}(0.02) \\\\  & =(0.02)+(0.98)(0.02)+(0.98)^{2}(0.02)+(0.98)^{3}(0.02) \\\\  & =[1+0.98+(0.98)^{2}+(0.98)^{3}](0.02) \\\\  & ≈0.078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186163956 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163961 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186163962 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163967 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164820 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164821 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164822 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164823 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164824 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539484 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539506 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539516 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539719 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186164819 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539760 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186164819 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539484 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186166736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540027 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186166737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540098 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186166738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540036 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186166955 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540067 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167151 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼Po(11)$ given below, compute $P(X < 10)$.\n\n| $x$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.1432$ | $0.2320$ | $0.3405$ | $0.4599$ | $0.5793$ | $0.6887$ | $0.7813$ |" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186167151 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(11)$ given below, compute $P(X<10).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.1432\\,$ | $\\,0.2320\\,$ | $\\,0.3405\\,$ | $\\,0.4599\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.6887\\,$ | $\\,0.7813\\,$ |" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167151 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<10) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,9\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 9) \\\\  & =F(9) \\\\  & =0.3405.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167152 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼Po(6)$ given below, compute $P(2 < X \\le 5)$.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0620$ | $0.1512$ | $0.2851$ | $0.4457$ | $0.6063$ | $0.7440$ | $0.8472$ |" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186167152 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(6)$ given below, compute $P(2<X\\le 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0620\\,$ | $\\,0.1512\\,$ | $\\,0.2851\\,$ | $\\,0.4457\\,$ | $\\,0.6063\\,$ | $\\,0.7440\\,$ | $\\,0.8472\\,$ |" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167152 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(2<X\\le 5) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(2<X\\le 5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.4457-0.0620 \\\\  & =0.3837.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167270 1071 "Let $F(x) = x^{2} - 2x$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$, and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x ∈ (- π, π)$. What is $f(x)$ for $x ∈ (- π, 0)$?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186167270 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186167270 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186172105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540024 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186172105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540099 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186172106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540111 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186172129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540042 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186172129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540051 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174131 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174132 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174133 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174134 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174135 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539545 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539695 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539711 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539819 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539875 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174130 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539819 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174677 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174678 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174679 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174680 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174681 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539541 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539575 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539748 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539804 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539915 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186174676 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539748 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175818 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y$, as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186175818 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y,$  as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175818 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\nf(y)=3y-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n3y - y^{2} &= y \\\\\n2y - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny(2 - y) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}y([3y - y^{2}] - [y])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}y(2y - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}(2y^{2} - y^{3})dy \\\\\n&= 2π(2\\int_{0}^{2}y^{2}dy - \\int_{0}^{2}y^{3}dy) \\\\\n&= 2π(2 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right|_{0}^{2} - \\left.\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π(2(\\frac{8}{3} - 0) - (\\frac{16}{4} - 0)) \\\\\n&= 2π(\\frac{16}{3} - 4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186175818 1074 "First, let $f(y)=3y-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([3y-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{16}{3}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175819 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2$, $x=y$, and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fa/fa2a775faed9c425a3b94e417b761d11486b4f41dae3129d9c9e016c28a652f0.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186175819 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2,$ $x=y,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/16/16a30b8310128aa588b3278b5a0984193ce57cbb5ea9118fc167a636487363b8.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175819 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\nf(y)=2-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n2 - y^{2} &= y \\\\\ny^{2} + y - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(y + 2)(y - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= -2, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/4c/4cc38a21168a86d2b64289e9c7877fbff4bf976b7214ed4befeb9644894f6172.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y([2 - y^{2}] - [y])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y(2 - y - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186175819 1074 "First, let $f(y)=2-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/f6/f69e2c1cd3b099a4ba192357db32274867c4f296e2c34e31e5110ee31b270317.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540025 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540031 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540103 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540106 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540107 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540032 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540053 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540083 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540084 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176158 1050 660.0 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176312 1071 "![](images/66/66ab1a3e2e7244a9b3e5fc9bda814736194c28dd9438fbb97086e99be4633261.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176312 1071 "![](images/66/66ab1a3e2e7244a9b3e5fc9bda814736194c28dd9438fbb97086e99be4633261.png)\n\nEvaluate $∮_(C)(\\sqrt{x} - 4x^{2}y)dx + (e^{y} + 4xy^{2})dy$, where the path $C$ is shown above." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186176312 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/70/709d108b2b992d1714c7c0b3bef26f52e965c2467f38d05103cd45f4f9bc821b.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{y}^{2}-(-4{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176403 1071 "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x = 5y - 2y^{2}$ and $x = y$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186176403 1071 "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $x=y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176403 1074 "First, let $f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 5y-2{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d8/d873c0acb12392e4ea40f761c72c1903a91c4f0e9fef04b586a71697957e2401.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([5y-2{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176485 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/45/459a51f3c408a9c647b3be6b978267a6ce724e747118fbe897275c370d2bb6d6.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186176485 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/45/459a51f3c408a9c647b3be6b978267a6ce724e747118fbe897275c370d2bb6d6.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186176983 1064 "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $x=f(y)$ on the right, $x=g(y)$ on the left, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$, as shown below. We assume that\n\n$$\nf(y) \\geq g(y)\n$$\n\nfor all $y \\in [c,d]$.\n\n![](images/9a/9a3970253f710223a77fe43f717df908fc20f27950744166ea7197897d64936d.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([f(y)] - [g(y)])dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186176983 1064 "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $x=f(y)$ on the right, $x=g(y)$ on the left, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d,$ as shown below. We assume that $f(y) \\geq g(y)$ for all $y \\in [c,d].$ \n\n![](images/9a/9a3970253f710223a77fe43f717df908fc20f27950744166ea7197897d64936d.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([f(y)]-[g(y)])\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177910 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177911 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177912 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177914 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539535 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539560 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539726 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186177909 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539821 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186177909 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539535 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186180040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186180040 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + (y - x)j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = 3\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π)$." 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186180040 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186180040 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x+y=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y-x=3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186198592 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\,\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-1,\\,uv,\\,3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-6,\\,2u⟩.$*" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186198592 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\,\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-1,\\,uv,\\,3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-6,\\,2u⟩.$*" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186198602 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 131941