539828 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186205341 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539864 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186205341 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539914 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186205341 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539828 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540069 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540057 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540068 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540048 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208930 1071 "Consider the function $L: R^{2} → R$ defined by $L(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(Ax - b)^{T}(Ax - b)$ where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is $∇_{x}L(x)$ at $x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186208930 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186208930 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)={y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186233065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539961 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186234385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539946 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186234385 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/ea/ea0502e8b261358c7b55c60df02474c5b39a75cbb104d6ba3efb40300294ef56.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}Y=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{XY}{YZ}=\\frac{XY}{7}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XY.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{Y{Z}^{2}-X{Z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}Y & =\\frac{XY}{7}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 false] [17592186234385 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/ea/ea0502e8b261358c7b55c60df02474c5b39a75cbb104d6ba3efb40300294ef56.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{XY}{YZ}=\\frac{X