 the tank is filled with gas. If the fuel costs $$0.0016$ per cubic centimeter, how much would it cost to fill the tank to capacity?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540022 1072 #uuid "014f97dd-12f1-54e9-8347-3c53a77026ab" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540022 1074 "The length of $7\\,\\text{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents one-fifth of the tank's side length. Therefore, the length of each side of the tank is\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 7=35\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the cube is a rectangular prism that is $35\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $35\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $35-7=28\\,\\text{cm}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,w=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,h=28\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=35\\cdot 35\\cdot 28=34\\,300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to fill the tank to capacity, it would cost\n\n$$\n34\\,300\\cdot 0.0016=$54.88.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539958 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540023 1069 "q-47211" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540023 1071 "$6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}y)=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540023 1072 #uuid "0be7ea7e-f479-5c43-ae90-b193d0f73efd" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540023 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =6({log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+6{log}_{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540021 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540024 1069 "q-159642" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540024 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=30$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540024 1072 #uuid "12431c38-5ac6-59aa-ba15-2add6471e563" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540024 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540012 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540025 1069 "q-107142" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540025 1071 "![](images/92/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540025 1072 #uuid "1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540025 1074 "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/02/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539939 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540026 1069 "q-222074" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540026 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-8x-7$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540026 1072 #uuid "164d94c5-b0eb-592e-af54-44af80279820" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540026 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=1$ and $b=-8$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-8)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=4.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539984 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540027 1069 "q-224994" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540027 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(7)$ given below, find the value of $P(X\\le 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0009\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0296\\,$ | $\\,0.0818\\,$ | $\\,0.1730\\,$ | $\\,0.3007\\,$ | $\\,0.4497\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\le 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540027 1072 #uuid "17398103-0726-57d3-aad8-24938a444c94" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540027 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =F(5) \\\\  & =0.3007.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539920 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539937 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1069 "q-264870" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1071 "Given the function \n$f(x;w)=2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $x={[\\begin{aligned}-2,\\,8\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1072 #uuid "1a79f623-fca8-50cb-ac85-6ca0845384fa" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540028 1074 "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}(2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}(2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2})=-5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $x={[\\begin{aligned}-2,\\,8\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ -5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540010 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540029 1069 "q-355015" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540029 1071 "John wonders what bottle of juice to buy from three different types available at the convenience store:\n\n- Bottle $A$ contains $20$ ounces, and costs $$3.00\\,.$\n\n- Bottle $B$ contains $32$ ounces, and costs $$4.80\\,.$\n\n- Bottle $C$ contains $10$ ounces, and costs $$1.80\\,.$\n\nWhich bottle of juice is the best deal?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540029 1072 #uuid "1ced6944-9e50-5164-8d33-69e2545adde3" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540029 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bottles. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $A$,  we get\n\n$$\n3.00÷20=0.15\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $A$ is $$0.15$  per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $B$,  we get\n\n$$\n4.80÷32=0.15\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $B$ is $$0.15$  per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $C$,  we get\n\n$$\n1.80÷10=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $C$ is $$0.18$  per ounce.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bottles $A$ and $B$ have the lowest unit price. Therefore, bottles $A$ and $B$ are the best deal." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539926 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540030 1069 "q-118846" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540030 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{ft},$ $\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{ft},$ and $2\\,\\text{ft},$ respectively?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540030 1072 #uuid "1e1eb62e-08d0-56cd-999d-56094605f117" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540030 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{ft},\\,w=\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{ft},\\,h=2\\,\\text{ft}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot 2=2\\sqrt{50}=10\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539947 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540031 1069 "q-107304" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540031 1071 "![](images/5e/5eac1b1d21378d64b90fc2463eb7664b30224594ba5d63aa70a7c412097012a0.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540031 1072 #uuid "1e2fc7b4-11f3-5f1c-807e-b40bd8df74a7" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540031 1074 "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(3-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/84/84e62b99598649ac48d55171b0d6bf7e5d26e38a6ad94c46ef9c89145e60e29a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y([1+y]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (3(\\frac{27}{3}-0)-(\\frac{81}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539952 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540032 1069 "q-107218" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540032 1071 "![](images/89/8957a7db313379597a21e6b51d6c4493058af207a2d8edd5fafb8e549ba45aae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1+{y}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540032 1072 #uuid "1ede84af-72eb-557a-81fe-42dab11d2478" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540032 1074 "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1+{y}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1+{y}^{3} \\\\ y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y(1-y)(1+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/b9/b91dedd1282ce1e9d8546bc6f7c52eba8741a2cd49613a03ef62866a9ba91b0c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1+{y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+y]-[1+{y}^{3}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539943 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540033 1069 "q-122173" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540033 1071 "![](images/bd/bdc0ea077dce75d6de1250ee6cf2e6dbd119b5124330c9a92edc071e475d0755.png)\n\nFind the sine of $∠U.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540033 1072 #uuid "1fdf769a-b9f2-5675-a0ff-59f0108ca843" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540033 1074 "Recall the definition of sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡U=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{ST}{SU}=\\frac{ST}{12.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $ST.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =\\sqrt{S{U}^{2}-T{U}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12.5)^{2}-(7.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{156.25-56.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡U & =\\frac{ST}{12.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{12.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539982 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539986 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1069 "q-264855" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y;a,b)=ax+by,$ compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{a,b}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1072 #uuid "209023bf-0612-560b-b7ed-a33687740998" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540034 1074 "To compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(ax+by)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(ax+by)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,b}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539953 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540035 1069 "q-352750" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540035 1071 "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}}{{e}^{n+2}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{6}$ is the sixth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{6}|<k$ for some real number $k.$\n\nFind the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540035 1072 #uuid "26d0f888-4d20-5d31-b520-f4c4f17a1d7e" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540035 1074 "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{n+2}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{e}^{n+2}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{{(-1)}^{7}}{{e}^{7+2}}|=\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540018 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540036 1069 "q-225086" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540036 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(9)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 8).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0550\\,$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,0.4557\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\ge 8)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540036 1072 #uuid "27647abc-efb3-57c0-813d-ce357897815d" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540036 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 8)=P(X>7).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 8) & =P(X>7) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 7) \\\\  & =1-F(7) \\\\  & =1-0.3239 \\\\  & =0.6761.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539989 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540037 1069 "q-53011" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540037 1071 "![](images/6a/6ada3cc0fcd02f363da922e9537d3f832cd616886da84f63e65085bd271aa3e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the rectangle above?\n\nI. $∠BCD$ is not a right angle.\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are parallel.\n\nIII. $BC=CD.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540037 1072 #uuid "2b8aa940-8e5f-5fd6-a1b0-b18405dff657" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540037 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. A rectangle is a parallelogram where all angles are right. Therefore, $∠BCD$ is a right angle.\n\n- Statement II is true. The opposite sides of a rectangle are parallel, so $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are parallel.\n\n- Statement III is false. Opposite sides of a rectangle are congruent, however, $BC$ and $CD$ are adjacent and we see that $BC\\ne CD.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539931 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540038 1069 "q-78777" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540038 1071 "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(4,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(-x)?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540038 1072 #uuid "30ee1395-5a0d-5715-816e-6b81b1745415" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540038 1074 "The transformation $y=f(-x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(4,1)$ lies $4$ units to the *right* from the $y$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $4$ units to the *left* from the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7e76fb2b9821c8bb116740381248e16d8331fbfc2cce4314984fe39bbfc723e7.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(-4,1)$ must lie on the graph of $y=f(-x).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539968 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540039 1069 "q-53031" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540039 1071 "![](images/fe/fe3724e266b68939b97f07fd7f428173ff0693b0d309d085187e44a45ba466ac.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a kite?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540039 1072 #uuid "33255947-d8b8-558c-b5b9-74099a601ba7" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540039 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a trapezoid. It doesn't have two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, therefore, it is not a kite.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a rectangle. Since it doesn't have two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, it is not a kite.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Therefore, it has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a parallelogram. Any two adjacent sides are not congruent with each other, therefore, it is not a kite.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a kite." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539936 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540040 1069 "q-91921" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540040 1071 "Anne needs to buy copper wire, the unit price for the copper wire is $$0.08$ per foot. How many feet of cooper wire can Anne buy with $$1?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540040 1072 #uuid "33438ac4-91a4-539b-acda-ce62d65ce1c2" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540040 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$0.08$ per foot (dollars per foot). To find the amount of copper wire that Anne can buy for $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in feet per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n0.08\\,\\text{dollars per foot}\\,\\to \\,1÷0.08=12.5\\,\\text{feet per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $12.5$ feet per dollar. Therefore, Anne can buy $12.5$ feet of copper wire." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539950 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540041 1069 "q-91920" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540041 1071 "Louis visits a snack shop, the unit price for nuts is $$8$ per pound. How many pounds of nuts can Louis buy for $$1?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540041 1072 #uuid "344d25f1-e189-5dcd-8b33-971f6adcaf13" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540041 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$8$ per pound (dollars per pound). To find how many pounds of nuts she can buy for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in pounds per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n8\\,\\text{dollars per pound}\\,\\to \\,1÷8=0.125\\,\\text{pounds per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.125$ pounds per dollar. Therefore, Louis can buy $0.125$ pounds of nuts for $1$ dollar." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540014 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540042 1069 "q-159641" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540042 1071 "It is known that the thickness of ceramic tiles from suppliers X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=1.6\\,\\text{mm}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=1.2\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. A construction contractor wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier Y. He constructs a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the contractor sampled $20$ tiles from supplier X and $18$ tiles from supplier Y and found that their mean thicknesses are $18.8\\,\\text{mm}$ and $18.5\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540042 1072 #uuid "34a9b0ce-4c8f-5634-aaa8-208faeda7581" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540042 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=18.8-18.5=0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{1.6}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18}}≈0.894\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 0.894\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.3-0.894,\\,0.3+0.894)=(-0.594,\\,1.194)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540001 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540043 1069 "q-250785" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540043 1071 "If ${log}_{5}⁡s=4$ and ${log}_{5}⁡t=5,$ then ${log}_{5}⁡(st)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540043 1072 #uuid "35a5df48-5462-5c58-bbc0-60fbb99c3f4a" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540043 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(st) & ={log}_{5}⁡s+{log}_{5}⁡t \\\\  & =4+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539998 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540044 1069 "q-99909" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540044 1071 "Express $8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ as a single logarithm." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540044 1072 #uuid "36bfe507-bf36-5e20-b82e-5099f50d12ef" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540044 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =8(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539991 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540045 1069 "q-66743" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540045 1071 "Express ${log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2$ as a single logarithm." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540045 1072 #uuid "3a84d4a4-1873-5e3e-810d-d5ca7ce87d53" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540045 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(8\\cdot 3)+{log}_{6}⁡2 \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(24\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539945 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540046 1069 "q-107606" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540046 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m},$ $4\\,\\text{m},$ and $3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m},$ respectively?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540046 1072 #uuid "3d05a680-5939-5ca9-9607-1df6158bf7b7" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540046 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m},\\,w=4\\,\\text{m},\\,h=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}=24\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539951 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540047 1069 "q-107630" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540047 1071 "A storage center has ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular solid. Each unit is $9\\,\\text{ft}$ long, $24\\,\\text{ft}$ wide, and $8\\,\\text{ft}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the units?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540047 1072 #uuid "3e2801a7-e084-5269-a941-751cceb06faf" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540047 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9,\\,w=24,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=9\\cdot 24\\cdot 8=1\\,728\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the ten storage units is\n\n$$\n1\\,728\\cdot 10=17\\,280\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539977 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540048 1069 "q-269525" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540048 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540048 1072 #uuid "3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540048 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539927 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540049 1069 "q-25070" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540049 1071 "![](images/6e/6eda76bbacb2cbebfe9a798b957c013dc34f684b4d866488541af759b25972b7.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{cot}⁡D=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540049 1072 #uuid "4bfcc405-f2a2-5e8e-bff5-6d6acca08903" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540049 1074 "We compute the cotangent of the angle $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡D=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{opposite}}=\\frac{15}{112}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539940 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540050 1069 "q-108103" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540050 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=10$ and $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=2$, then $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540050 1072 #uuid "4ea44857-e35f-51f3-a0fe-716b02fb3232" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540050 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =10+2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539956 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540051 1069 "q-159626" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540051 1071 "The weights of the wooden and steel chairs produced by a furniture company have standard deviations of $2\\,\\text{lb}$ and $1\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively. The company wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of weights for the wooden chairs is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of weights for the steel chairs. They construct a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the company sampled $100$ wooden chairs and $150$ steel chairs and found that their mean weights are $10\\,\\text{lb}$ and $9\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540051 1072 #uuid "5391d4bc-fe22-5a3b-85bc-6502aec7782e" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540051 1074 "Notice that \n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=10-9=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{150}}≈0.423\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1-0.423,\\,1+0.423)=(0.577,\\,1.423)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539919 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540052 1069 "q-66752" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540052 1071 "Express $5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5$ as a single logarithm." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540052 1072 #uuid "56e99923-e71e-5783-b293-6f220455cbaa" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540052 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5 & =5({log}_{3}⁡4+{log}_{3}⁡5) \\\\  & =5{log}_{3}⁡(4\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =5{log}_{3}⁡20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5=5{log}_{3}⁡20.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539988 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540053 1069 "q-126191" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540053 1071 "![](images/da/dadb1e7740ee89fbbf9cffe69501ebd7be655ec9aaee30071b09fc767e80092c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540053 1072 #uuid "5dabe85e-85d9-5f83-8489-ec7933e9dac9" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540053 1074 "First, let $f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+2y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-y)(1+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d9/d9f7cf87c59b7a0fc9600178795524f35a72b12466385e63eafa35c1fdb3537c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+2y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1+2y-{y}^{2}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539934 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540054 1069 "q-66760" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540054 1071 "${log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540054 1072 #uuid "64aa673c-cb55-5490-a5e1-8f5f8d78ed0e" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540054 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})={log}_{2}⁡32+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $32={2}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡32={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2}) & ={log}_{2}⁡32+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539993 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540055 1069 "q-106547" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540055 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}+4x-9.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540055 1072 #uuid "64f1282c-c0b1-511f-b876-dbf03d7ec8a1" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540055 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-2$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(-4)} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=1.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=1$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{x}^{2}+4x-9 \\\\  & =-2(1)^{2}+4(1)-9 \\\\  & =-2+4-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(1,-7).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539957 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540004 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1069 "q-264852" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1071 "Given the quartic function $f(x;a,b,c)=a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c{x}^{2},$ compute ${∇}_{b,c}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{b,c}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1072 #uuid "69b033a7-d872-59bc-ae3f-9a5bfb186950" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540056 1074 "To compute ${∇}_{b,c}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $b$ and $c.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c{x}^{2})={x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂c} & =\\frac{∂}{∂c}(a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{b,c}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂b} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂c}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539959 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539994 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1069 "q-276043" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1072 #uuid "6d939167-bbdf-5642-a79d-3234f69c9c09" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540057 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+2 \\\\ 0-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540007 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540058 1069 "q-53038" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540058 1071 "![](images/b1/b15000ac7f2de0770140ba289a8f5224433bfb6570d985ab184eacba016c97c4.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rectangle?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540058 1072 #uuid "6ffd60c0-4297-5e49-a178-11a94ccb47c6" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540058 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rectangle is a quadrilateral where all angles are right with two pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. It has no right angles, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, all its angles are right, so it is a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rectangle since all its angles are right.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. Since there are no pairs of parallel sides, it is not a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are rectangles. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not rectangles." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539933 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540059 1069 "q-251896" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540059 1071 "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $12$ | $14$ | $9$ | $17$ | $15$ | $10$ | $14$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $15$ | $18$ | $11$ | $10$ | $20$ | $15$ | $16$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=1\\,231,$ calculate ${S}_{xx}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540059 1072 #uuid "72d27b98-5424-5f73-97fc-8f2eaa4b8548" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540059 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=12+\\,14+\\,9+\\,17+\\,15+\\,10+\\,14=91.\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=1\\,231.$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=1\\,231-\\frac{1}{7}(91)^{2}=1231-1\\,183=48.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539992 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540060 1069 "q-107608" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540060 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $9\\,\\text{ft},$ $4\\,\\text{ft},$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ respectively?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540060 1072 #uuid "76532f80-93cf-5f33-8bd0-322c7a249f9a" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540060 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9\\,\\text{ft},\\,w=4\\,\\text{ft},\\,h=5\\,\\text{ft}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=9\\cdot 4\\cdot 5=180\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539969 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540061 1069 "q-67554" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540061 1071 "![](images/de/de81dfe9ea7efd41a7ca5b576452c62491ab438dbb0b75eb7c7a6c13a903fe6f.png)\n\nDetermine the cosine of $∠Q.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540061 1072 #uuid "7705945a-9fcd-5876-a71d-6b7d8aba9b73" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540061 1074 "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡Q=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PQ}=\\frac{QR}{8.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $QR.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8.5)^{2}-(7.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72.25-56.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡Q & =\\frac{QR}{8.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540002 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540062 1069 "q-65381" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540062 1071 "![](images/e9/e980e9415d52338c90eb5c167a32b02815237b1554f9aea06f831e88db6f3777.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540062 1072 #uuid "789a361b-1deb-51ca-88a0-26707116d922" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540062 1074 "Remember that $cot$ is the reciprocal of $tan.$ So first we need to compute tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{PR}{QR}=\\frac{3}{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1}=2.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539924 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540063 1069 "q-121995" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540063 1071 "![](images/18/182a88f3f8f379dd35228cbb6ebe5c34d9761608a6a222710f50d8f272d3cf1e.png)\n\nCalculate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡R.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540063 1072 #uuid "78a31293-8e99-54e0-b99f-691a4b369ca7" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540063 1074 "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡R=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{2}{PR}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $PR.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}+Q{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡R & =\\frac{2}{PR}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540011 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540064 1069 "q-118851" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540064 1071 "The height of a rectangular solid is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}$ and its volume is $24\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.$ If the base  (length × width) of the solid is a square, find the length of the solid." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540064 1072 #uuid "78b1f6d8-2df3-5686-9ee0-696c5e38afe8" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540064 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSince the base of the rectangular solid is a square, we have that \n\n$$\nl=w.\n$$\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=24\\sqrt{3},\\,h=2\\sqrt{3},\\,w=l\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =lwh \\\\ V & ={l}^{2}h \\\\ 24\\sqrt{3} & ={l}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =12 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539979 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540009 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1069 "q-264896" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1071 "Given the function $f(x;w)={w}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2},$ compute ${∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f).$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f)=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1072 #uuid "869ee47c-102a-5b9f-9e3a-e75445c641bf" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540065 1074 "First, we compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2}=2{w}_{2}{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2}=({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}}=2{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}}=2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}}=2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2} & 2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\ 2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539935 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539938 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539985 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540015 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1069 "q-264885" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$, compute ${∇}_{x,z}({∇}_{x,z}\\,f).$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{x,z}({∇}_{x,z}f)=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1072 #uuid "892266dd-4065-579e-8406-1b3b7c58257b" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540066 1074 "First, we take the variable subset gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}yz \\\\ xy\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)=0\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz)=y \\\\  & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}({∇}_{x,z}\\,f) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & y \\\\ y & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539928 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540067 1069 "q-341407" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540067 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540067 1072 #uuid "899a46d0-c395-5e11-8fbc-04b90f756929" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540067 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{3}-2(-x)^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540003 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540006 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1069 "q-276044" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1072 #uuid "8b961be3-a61d-5fd5-a33f-daa3b859082d" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540068 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0-10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539967 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540069 1069 "q-265760" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540069 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540069 1072 #uuid "8ea8eb91-918f-5aeb-b452-a90611b4dd2a" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540069 1074 "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539999 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540070 1069 "q-10230" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540070 1071 "![](images/37/37e62bc959e4a71abdeb9efab170916a914d687d5c02e7850d01eb281acd131b.png)\n\nDetermine the height of the rectangular solid above given that its volume is $702\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540070 1072 #uuid "90da9001-fcc8-57a5-93d8-6080245acd79" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540070 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=702,\\,l=13,\\,w=6\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}702 & =13\\cdot 6\\cdot h \\\\ 702 & =78h \\\\ h & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height is $9\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539960 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540071 1069 "q-118872" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540071 1071 "A person has seven identical boxes in the form of a rectangular solid. Each box is $10\\,\\text{in}$ long, $8\\,\\text{in}$ wide, and $8\\,\\text{in}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the boxes?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540071 1072 #uuid "90edfdf5-24c3-54cd-8862-63c978f95f47" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540071 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one box.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=10,\\,w=8,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=10\\cdot 8\\cdot 8=640\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the seven storage boxes is\n\n$$\n640\\cdot 7=4\\,480\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540008 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540072 1069 "q-107620" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540072 1071 "![](images/ab/abec41d02ba71b2e94d7b2c3cd92f552ddab907de494719a22706b568a588be4.png)\n\nDetermine the length of the rectangular solid above given that its volume is $84\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540072 1072 #uuid "91cc3a07-1501-5243-8832-8d928c7de8f2" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540072 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=84,\\,w=3\\sqrt{2},\\,h=\\sqrt{8}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84 & =l\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8} \\\\ 84 & =3\\sqrt{16}l \\\\ 84 & =12l \\\\ l & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $7\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539925 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540073 1069 "q-85889" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540073 1071 "A lottery decides that $3$ out of every $20$ lottery tickets will win a special prize. If a player wants to win a special prize, what is the probability of finding a prize before purchasing their $5$th ticket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540073 1072 #uuid "934447c8-2b56-5fbe-8d1c-94052a2dad02" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540073 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of tickets purchased until a special prize is found. On average, the chance of finding a special prize on a single ticket is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of tickets that the player should purchase before they find a special prize can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a special prize before the $5$th ticket is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{4-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}+(0.85)^{3}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.478\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539949 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540074 1069 "q-122902" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540074 1071 "![](images/a9/a9003434f721485b5ee3eb2ed417d0c4c68514b98060f6149bc974270e55411a.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡P=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540074 1072 #uuid "9728a2a0-30c7-53d5-836b-39033ca5b896" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540074 1074 "Remember that $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{QR}{15}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{R}^{2}-P{Q}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{QR}{15}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\frac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡P={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540000 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540075 1069 "q-67413" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540075 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540075 1072 #uuid "9929bc33-2631-5ab2-871b-80ee305fc700" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540075 1074 "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+5=3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =3 \\\\ x+5-5 & =3-5 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539996 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540076 1069 "q-108100" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540076 1071 "Express $4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8$ as a single logarithm." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540076 1072 #uuid "9ea8b2b1-a8ee-55af-9a04-8ea83c2d0aaa" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540076 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8 & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+\\operatorname{log}⁡8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\cdot 4\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8=4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539980 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540077 1069 "q-352755" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540077 1071 "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-2)}^{n}}{(n+1)!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{5}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540077 1072 #uuid "a26aabae-4ded-5132-8e5c-c91af894cefb" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540077 1074 "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n}}{(n+1)!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-2)}^{n}}{(n+1)!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $5$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{5}|<|{b}_{6}|=|\\frac{{(-2)}^{6}}{(6+1)!}|=\\frac{64}{5040}=\\frac{4}{315}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{4}{315}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539955 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540078 1069 "q-96016" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540078 1071 "$\\int (4-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540078 1072 #uuid "a2700145-f14c-508c-9486-f4eea106d539" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540078 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta )={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\int {sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}\\theta +\\int (-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\int \\,\\text{d}\\theta -3\\int {sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta -3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539941 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540079 1069 "q-83612" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540079 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}-1}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.1.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540079 1072 #uuid "aa9e57c4-993f-58af-8f8c-2c21d29224b2" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540079 1074 "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-1}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}-1}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 2$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{(i+1)^{2}-1}| & \\le 0.1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{i}^{2}+2i+1-1} & \\le \\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{i}^{2}+2i} & \\le \\frac{1}{10} \\\\ {i}^{2}+2i & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539918 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540080 1069 "q-85881" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540080 1071 "An experiment consists of tossing a fair coin until it comes up heads. What is the probability that we get our first head on the $3$rd toss? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540080 1072 #uuid "adc9ba22-54ae-50d6-b1c5-428a57977aea" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540080 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the coin is tossed until it comes up heads. Since the chance of getting a head in every toss is $50%,$ the number of tosses until it comes up heads can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=50%=\\frac{50}{100}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that head appears in the $3$rd toss is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}(0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539970 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540081 1069 "q-122045" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540081 1071 "In a right triangle $△FGH,$ $∠H$ is a right angle, $FH=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $FG=6.$ Find the cosine of $∠G.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540081 1072 #uuid "aec65431-c1ff-5f84-a471-b95024c34089" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540081 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/e5/e585e89d11dea316c5483eeb2b2fc4750084409651ecf838e65a0d09e7bcc97f.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡G=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{GH}{FG}=\\frac{GH}{6}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $GH.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GH & =\\sqrt{F{G}^{2}-F{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36-8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{28} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡G & =\\frac{GH}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{7}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539963 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540082 1069 "q-222079" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540082 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}+10x-3$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540082 1072 #uuid "b00cf238-5726-569e-8376-bed95c1380a1" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540082 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=-2$ and $b=10$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{10}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539921 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540083 1069 "q-126325" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540083 1071 "![](images/b6/b61c628a012f6bde3144254b521e11f8803e089cd7f83f2c19a1255cf22c0e3a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}},$ $x=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540083 1072 #uuid "b0ffc976-87a0-51e6-8ff5-4a0a8b70546a" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540083 1074 "First, let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}{y}^{2}+y+3 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2}-4y-12 & =0 \\\\ (5y+6)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/12/122a21e72dc7473cc34ed3ece0325294eadcc86cbe8a5b91e4dfe4be77b369c0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}y+1])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539954 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540084 1069 "q-106122" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540084 1071 "![](images/30/30aa4c5e72919d09a0a2323dc8af90a73810047857f3c89fe9c3005a10c195ae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540084 1072 #uuid "b61e8b5a-f6d1-5f01-ad3a-3725586d9405" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540084 1074 "First, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cb/cbfd6c409590e6b6d350b5ae8f6531944467e90991b17582febe228a6a8dfdb2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539948 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540085 1069 "q-13389" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540085 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=3{x}^{2}-18x-3.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540085 1072 #uuid "b9ff66d7-f50d-5b18-a934-a31f261f86c6" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540085 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3$ and $b=-18$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-18)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-18)}{6} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}-18x-3 \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-18(3)-3 \\\\  & =27-54-3 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,-30).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539972 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540086 1069 "q-104031" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540086 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{2}^{n}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.01.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540086 1072 #uuid "bb487298-b519-5edb-8e52-6ad72f9356fb" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540086 1074 "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{2}^{n}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 1$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{{2}^{i+1}}| & \\le 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{i+1}} & \\le \\frac{1}{100} \\\\ 100 & \\le {2}^{i+1} \\\\ 100 & \\le 2\\cdot {2}^{i} \\\\ {2}^{i} & \\ge 50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540016 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540087 1069 "q-85898" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540087 1071 "A professional soccer team participates in a championship. On average, the team has a $20%$ chance of losing each game. What is the probability that the team plays at least $4$ games without losing (i.e., the first loss occurs after the $4$th game)?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540087 1072 #uuid "bc079e51-1277-52af-9686-51765572e614" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540087 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of games played until they lose their first game. Since the chance of losing each game is $20%,$ the number of games played before the team loses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=20%=\\frac{20}{100}=0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.2).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.2)^{x-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{x-1}(0.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>4)=1-P(X\\le 4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{1-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{2-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{4-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.2)+(0.8)(0.2)+(0.8)^{2}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3}(0.2) \\\\  & =[1+0.8+(0.8)^{2}+(0.8)^{3}](0.2) \\\\  & =0.5904.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team will play at least $4$ games before their first loss\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.5904 \\\\  & =0.4096.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539997 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540088 1069 "q-118847" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540088 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $7\\,\\text{cm},$ $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm},$ and $2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540088 1072 #uuid "bc3873ba-541e-5572-9cfe-0e10d2402ed6" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540088 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=7\\,\\text{cm},\\,w=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm},\\,h=2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=7\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=14\\sqrt{25}=70\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539971 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540089 1069 "q-106548" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540089 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-4{x}^{2}-24x-10.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540089 1072 #uuid "c18c5b5e-e1be-5b37-80b1-242d38a7007c" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540089 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ and $b=-24$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-24)}{2(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-24)}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4{x}^{2}-24x-10 \\\\  & =-4(-3)^{2}-24(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-36+72-10 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-3,26).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539976 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540090 1069 "q-78473" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540090 1071 "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(-x)?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540090 1072 #uuid "c1cdde3c-f636-5b56-ac5b-9b6bdec51038" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540090 1074 "The transformation $y=f(-x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(2,-4)$ lies $2$ units to the *right* from the $y$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $2$ units to the *left* from the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/45/456a145820b2a36693df572b49064332a18a2e79c35f2a0e3240b9995660c9a5.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(-2,-4)$ must lie on the graph of $y=f(-x).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540017 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540091 1069 "q-17836" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540091 1071 "![](images/6e/6e075462cab65c4070ed285e78d71fb2fafd7bcdcb898957456e83cddbf45c18.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(-x)?$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540091 1072 #uuid "c4f3f9b9-166f-5dd9-8c08-9d9ad8ea8dae" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540091 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=f(-x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $y$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed0d31d46df8c973f92ff5b8e23a3aaeeb782fa90c2f5abb75dcf88f2d7b7421.png)" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539922 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540092 1069 "q-66758" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540092 1071 "${log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540092 1072 #uuid "c529b9a8-1cea-5cb5-8b9e-8f76c8202de5" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540092 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})={log}_{4}⁡16+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16={4}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡16={log}_{4}⁡({4}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3}) & ={log}_{4}⁡16+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539990 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540020 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1069 "q-222087" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}-4x-3?$\n\nThe coordinates of the vertex are\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1072 #uuid "c719977b-e48f-55fa-971c-b6c55e94e98c" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540093 1074 "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=-4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-4)}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-4)}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-4x-3 \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}-4(-2)-3 \\\\  & =-4+8-3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-2,1).$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540019 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540094 1069 "q-250764" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540094 1071 "Write the following expression as a single logarithm.\n\n$5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540094 1072 #uuid "c7c12aa8-de03-59c9-ab5d-5e64e226cea3" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540094 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p\\cdot q\\cdot pq) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡({p}^{2}{q}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540013 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540095 1069 "q-106551" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540095 1071 "![](images/83/830944c0cff8aa0b2f1c58c9cd2ab7ae0f2560a9aa0e244796c0814ec99fd0c3.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=k{x}^{2}-4x-3,$ shown above." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540095 1072 #uuid "c9db5029-028a-58a7-87ff-11b09d5541ba" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540095 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=2,$ we will set our result equal to $2.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=k$ and $b=-4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-4)}{2(k)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=2,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k} & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539944 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540096 1069 "q-68910" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540096 1071 "In a store, there are three different types of cream cheese that come in boxes: \n\n- Box $A$ contains $1$ cup and costs $$1.96\\,.$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $1.2$ cups and costs $$2.40\\,.$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $1.5$ cups and costs $$2.94\\,.$\n\nWhich box of cream cheese is the best deal?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540096 1072 #uuid "cae6f157-aca0-5d45-812b-7c9eb68fbca5" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540096 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the price by the total number of cups.   \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of cups in box $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n1.96÷1=1.96\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $$1.96.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of cups in box $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n2.40÷1.2=2\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $B$ is $$2.00.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of cups in box $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n2.94÷1.5=1.96\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $$1.96$  too.\n\nFrom the above, we see that boxes $A$ and $C$  have the lowest unit price. Therefore, boxes $A$ and  $C$ are the best deal." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539995 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540097 1069 "q-68936" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540097 1071 "Nathan, a freelance journalist, charges $$0.04$ per word for his financial articles. How many words would it take for him to earn $1$ dollar?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540097 1072 #uuid "d5b928b2-4413-5ea5-b176-1122913e3fcf" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540097 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$0.04$ per word (dollars per word). To find how many words  it would take for him to earn $1$ dollar we need to find the unit rate in words per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n0.04\\,\\text{dollars per word}\\,\\to \\,1÷0.04=25\\,\\text{words per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $25$ words per dollar. Therefore, Nathan writes $25$ words for $1$ dollar." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539929 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540098 1069 "q-224966" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540098 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(4<X\\le 6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0138\\,$ | $\\,0.0424\\,$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ |\n\n$P(4<X\\le 6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540098 1072 #uuid "d8455b7b-7c5b-5bab-ac5b-3afa70d37c46" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540098 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(6) & =P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\})+P(X\\in \\{5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 4)+P(4<X\\le 6) \\\\  & =F(4)+P(4<X\\le 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 6) & =F(6)-F(4) \\\\  & =0.3134-0.0996 \\\\  & =0.2138.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539964 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540099 1069 "q-159643" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540099 1071 "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=28$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=19$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=11,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540099 1072 #uuid "db9cf9a1-6584-5405-8a36-efa72137f427" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540099 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-11=8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{32}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈3.118\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 8\\pm 3.118\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539966 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540100 1069 "q-67435" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540100 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540100 1072 #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540100 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{-4}) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539917 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540101 1069 "q-4650" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540101 1071 "![](images/7f/7f566dccaab9ea71af6fa627ce53bd400e97735d5b8c7500d63fddfdf2e7a8db.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡R=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540101 1072 #uuid "de4398c2-dc93-57fc-955e-876f667d9e66" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540101 1074 "We compute the secant of the angle $R$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡R=\\frac{\\text{hypotenuse}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{10}{6}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539930 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540102 1069 "q-118873" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540102 1071 "A shipping company delivered six identical packages in the form of a rectangular prism. Each package is $40\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $20\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the packages?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540102 1072 #uuid "e195bf20-65ce-5bd5-85a8-c18879f5cecb" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540102 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one package.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=40,\\,w=20,\\,h=10\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=40\\cdot 20\\cdot 10=8\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the six packages is\n\n$$\n8\\,000\\cdot 6=48\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539923 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540103 1069 "q-126377" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540103 1071 "![](images/48/482781bb21d011df5a9140b2b1fb0ebe1108f746c0055077d4f4e9a91fce735d.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=\\sqrt{2-x},$ $x={y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540103 1072 #uuid "e40037f0-3304-53a5-a4f4-7220f338664f" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540103 1074 "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)={y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/48/48905a174d688fdb3e099debb7840173a2ce047816003a5be9c089997196f9fb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=f(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540005 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540104 1069 "q-85897" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540104 1071 "In a town, blood tests are being carried out on all inhabitants to determine whether they have caught a virus. On average, $30%$ of the inhabitants test positive for the virus. What is the probability that the first positive test result will be made on the $3$rd test or later?" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540104 1072 #uuid "e49d61e7-549c-5568-9bc1-1e99e4e1790c" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540104 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found. Since the chance of a single patient being infected is $30%,$ the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7](0.3) \\\\  & =0.51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first positive patient on the $3$rd test or later is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.51 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539983 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540105 1069 "q-122005" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540105 1071 "In a right triangle $△LMN,$ $∠M$ is a right angle, $MN=\\sqrt{5}$ and $LM=\\sqrt{11}.$ Determine the cosine of $∠N.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540105 1072 #uuid "e6b2c405-4949-514d-b4c7-24a8d0d745b1" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540105 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/df/df7dcd343dd2676cb38b7df3c2d8cb599fed45b9c0b25194609effe2bf27a6f7.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡N=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{MN}{LN}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{LN}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $LN.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\sqrt{M{N}^{2}+L{M}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡N & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{LN}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539987 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540106 1069 "q-352199" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540106 1071 "![](images/1d/1d8d3dfa1134b7fd5861ff70e493ead2227dd2d322e8436e28a903e0c596a408.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540106 1072 #uuid "efbcafae-ea63-52c2-855e-a9cfa22be6a8" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540106 1074 "First, we write the second function in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 4y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/c5/c5a117e2af85e3380942939812442eac7b899a2259af79ad032654fc5662a767.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({4\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}-8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539973 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540107 1069 "q-352372" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540107 1071 "![](images/dc/dcb67e2d3fe9a413c02bb00b6d8cf7c0ac8825db1d360ab883f08baa3b6c2e9c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=7-y-{y}^{2},$ $x=5-{y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540107 1072 #uuid "f03e4c2d-a256-544b-8a7f-59a90059d73b" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540107 1074 "Now, let $f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=5-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 7-y-{y}^{2} & =5-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2-y & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cf/cfb914bf7ed0a0c6b9f784d46a6761cd97c352fcc4e82abb29a72dc12d8ab7ca.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=5-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([7-y-{y}^{2}]-[5-{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{4}{2}-0)-(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539978 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540108 1069 "q-222095" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540108 1071 "![](images/8e/8ea5d11ca8b92193517fa7082a466e2ece1f5c505e6881c70ce8ebebb5b93913.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=5{x}^{2}+kx-8,$ shown above.\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540108 1072 #uuid "f5ac6aea-bcfe-5a0e-83c5-58a351f6e0ff" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540108 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ we will set our result equal to $-2.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=5$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{10} & =-2 \\\\ -k & =-20 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539916 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540109 1069 "q-250763" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540109 1071 "Write the following expression as a single logarithm.\n\n$4\\operatorname{log}⁡t+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})=4\\operatorname{log}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540109 1072 #uuid "f62edb2c-be7a-51bc-a4d3-e51bf196542b" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540109 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡t+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3}) & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡t+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{1+2+3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539974 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540110 1069 "q-251858" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540110 1071 "Consider two sets of $39$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{39},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{39}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=663,\\,{S}_{yy}=1\\,092,\\,{S}_{xy}=975.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540110 1072 #uuid "f9b76f6d-a314-5376-893f-3c9706a4c31e" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540110 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=975.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{975}{39} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539932 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540111 1069 "q-159634" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540111 1071 "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=58$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=86$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=34$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=43,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540111 1072 #uuid "f9f98bfe-eda2-5f27-b0c5-b96f1c4792b0" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540111 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539962 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540112 1069 "q-66559" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540112 1071 "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2})=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540112 1072 #uuid "fba4c35a-120c-5bd8-b872-2cfc6815a465" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540112 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100={10}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡100=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539942 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540113 1069 "q-47212" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540113 1071 "$9\\operatorname{log}⁡(6z)=$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540113 1072 #uuid "fbe8f3d1-3c93-5a68-ac60-38c193b6c6b9" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540113 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{log}⁡(6z) & =9(\\operatorname{log}⁡6+\\operatorname{log}⁡z) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{log}⁡6+9\\operatorname{log}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539975 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540114 1069 "q-76563" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540114 1071 "In a right triangle $△OMN,$ $∠M$ is a right angle, $MN=1.8$ and $ON=3.$ Determine $\\operatorname{cos}⁡O.$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540114 1072 #uuid "fc60b6be-fd37-55aa-981e-4006b67e2bf9" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540114 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/5c/5c980bff90a516627618909a863b4919dbb10be1b674911cae663f3f7c54f3fb.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡O=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{OM}{ON}=\\frac{OM}{3}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $OM.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OM & =\\sqrt{O{N}^{2}-M{N}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-{1.8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-3.24} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5.76} \\\\  & =2.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡O & =\\frac{OM}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539965 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540115 1069 "q-251794" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540115 1071 "Consider two sets of $14$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{14},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{14}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}=157, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{y}_{i}=182, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=2\\,227.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${S}_{xy}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540115 1072 #uuid "fe1475b2-e88a-5639-9c34-aefc361e0ff5" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540115 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the statistic ${S}_{xy}$ be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & =2\\,227-\\frac{1}{14}(157)(182) \\\\  & =2\\,227-2\\,041 \\\\  & =186.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540116 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540140 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540116 1062 #uuid "04bd31c0-ddc2-5b9c-ad07-d73251e35927" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540116 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060376 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540117 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540176 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540117 1062 #uuid "075080f4-fbd5-5910-aa84-ddaafacb2543" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540117 1063 #edb/ref 17592186540115 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540118 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540153 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540118 1062 #uuid "0e09055a-8ec5-52c2-8a49-3ed30ccb5890" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540118 1063 #edb/ref 17592186540056 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540119 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540166 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186540119 